vibeee - this machine, and how to hold it

The shell runs programs, connects them with pipes, and sends output to
files:

  net                    run a command
  log | page             read one command's output through another
  smbios > /tmp/board    keep output in a file
  tools                  every command, with a line on each
  man <name>             this manual, one page per command

Tab completes command names and paths. Up and down walk the history.
The prompt arrow shows the last command's status: green succeeded, red
did not. Ctrl+C asks the running command to stop.

The filesystem:

  /bin       programs
  /etc       what the settings are when nobody has said otherwise
  /cfg       what was said otherwise; this is what outlives a reboot
  /home      user files, and where the shell starts
  /doc       this manual
  /media     mounted volumes appear here
  /tmp       scratch, cleared at reboot

The root filesystem is rebuilt from the boot medium every time, so /etc
and /tmp are set for one session. /cfg and /home are volumes of their
own on that medium: `cfg` writes to the first, and anything saved in the
second is still there next time. `disk` says where each one is.

A small kernel owns the screen, the disk, input and memory. Services
own the rest and restart on their own if they fail: platd speaks to
the firmware, devmgd decides which driver drives which device, netd
drives the network, sndd carries sound, usbd carries the USB bus, cfgd
keeps settings. `svc` lists them; `top` shows threads; `free` shows
memory; `driver` shows what the device manager matched.

What is plugged in arrives on its own. A USB disk appears in `disk`
and is mounted under /media; a USB keyboard types and a USB mouse
moves the pointer. `usb` lists the bus.

The wired network configures itself: an enabled interface asks DHCP
when its cable carries. `net` shows the result; `man net` describes
manual configuration.

Sound is a graph rather than a pipeline: programs and hardware are
nodes with ports, and links decide who hears whom. `tone` makes a
sound, `vol` sets the volume, and `patch` shows and changes the
wiring.

The boot carries a line of flags, set where the image is written. The
loader shows it and waits two seconds; press a key in that time and it
can be typed at:

  boot: verbose
        a key within two seconds changes it
  boot> verbose debug

Typing continues from the end of what is there, so the usual change is
a word added. Backspace takes one off; enter boots with what is shown.
A change lasts for that boot alone: the medium is not written to, so a
line that stops the machine booting is undone by starting it again.

The flags:

  verbose                the boot's narration on screen as well as in
                         the log, which `log` reads either way
  debug                  the tier beneath it, for chasing a fault
  nocolor                plain text, for a screen or a capture that
                         cannot carry it
  noautomount            mount nothing that is found, /cfg and /home
                         included; `mount` still attaches by hand, and
                         settings changed without /cfg are refused
  nofb                   stay in text mode; the loader otherwise sets a
                         graphics mode, which is what a program drawing
                         its own pixels needs. For a machine whose panel
                         the mode does not suit, and for reading a boot
                         that stops before the kernel's own display
                         driver takes over
  no.<service>           keep one service down for this boot, by the
                         name `svc` lists it under: `no.netd` boots
                         without the network. `nonet` and `nohw` are the
                         older spellings of `no.netd` and `no.devmgd`
  late.<service>         start it once the shell is on screen instead of
                         during the boot, so a service whose hardware
                         stops the machine does it under a prompt you
                         can read. `netlate` is the older spelling of
                         `late.netd`

`edit` changes a file and writes it back; `man edit` describes it. Long
output reads through `page`. The boot's own messages are under
`log`. `off` powers down; `reboot` restarts.
